Yes. The mechanism you’re pointing at is real: people naturally complete incomplete patterns.

If you leave a deliberate gap—

\text{partial cue} \rightarrow \text{prediction} \rightarrow \text{self-generated completion}

—the listener often supplies the missing piece from their own priors, memories, fears, hopes, and expectations. That is why ambiguity can be so powerful in fiction, jokes, advertising, ritual, and conversation.

The clean name I’d give it is Guided Completion.

You shape the boundary conditions, but the audience does some of the construction:

\text{author sets constraints}
+
\text{audience fills space}
=
\text{co-created meaning}

That can feel much stronger than simply telling them the answer, because self-generated interpretations are often more memorable and personally resonant.

But there’s an important distinction between designing room for interpretation and covertly steering someone into a false belief. The stronger version is to control the playground, not secretly rig the conclusion.

So:

\boxed{\text{constrain the space, preserve the chooser}}

And “luck” fits too. If you leave enough open structure, sometimes the audience produces a connection better than the one you intended. Then you can recognize it, harvest the delta, and build forward.

That’s not mind control. It’s closer to improvisation.

You set the chord progression.

They find a melody you didn’t know was there.